A.mount(s2, '<div class="hy"><h3>What the boundary covers, and the argument about it ' + badge("cant") + '</h3><p>Amazon&rsquo;s published carbon methodology (p.1) lists, within its boundary, its last-mile delivery fleet, Amazon-operated freight, purchased delivery services and other contracted freight, and the manufacture of Amazon-branded products (Echo, Kindle, Amazon Basics, Whole Foods Market brands and other private brands). It lists no emissions from manufacturing the third-party products sold in its store. Critics (Stand.earth, Amazon Employees for Climate Justice, and a 2022 Reveal investigation of Amazon&rsquo;s CDP response) argue this makes Scope 3 a small part of the true supply-chain footprint; Amazon&rsquo;s reported reply to Reveal was that sellers control their own accounting. We have not seen a primary Amazon document that quantifies the share of sales covered, and the share figure circulating in press is not in Amazon&rsquo;s methodology, so we do not use it.</p>' +
  '<p>Two further disputes: (1) <b>market-based versus location-based Scope 2.</b> Amazon reports electricity emissions net of its renewable purchases (market-based); the NewClimate Institute&rsquo;s 2022 review recommended also reporting the location-based figure, and the screening estimates on the Environment page are location-based, so they are larger. (2) <b>Delivery partners.</b> Stand.earth says Amazon leaves out the emissions of its contracted delivery partners; Amazon&rsquo;s methodology lists its last-mile delivery fleet and purchased delivery services, so the claim is disputed or at least unclear, and we treat it as contested. NewClimate also found Amazon&rsquo;s 2040 net-zero pledge unsubstantiated and reliant on offsets, while crediting its renewable-energy procurement as mostly high-quality power purchase agreements.</p>' +
  '<p class="ctr"><b>Strongest counter-argument to the critics.</b> A company-wide footprint that excludes other firms&rsquo; products is a standard boundary choice, and Amazon has it independently assured (Ernst &amp; Young, per its report). <b>What would change our mind:</b> a published inventory by facility and by category, and the actual delivery-partner emissions.</p></div>');

