A.mount(s3, '<p class="cap">Data: interp_env_transport_breakeven.csv. The three cases move several assumptions together in the direction that favors online, the middle, or the store; they are bracketing cases, not a probability range. Jaller and Pahwa&rsquo;s 2020 poster (San Francisco) found large CO2 savings if all shopping moved online with consolidated delivery, and small increases for an omni-channel mix; those are poster figures for full substitution.</p>');

/* 3f */
A.mount(s3, '<h3 class="gh" id="s3f">3f. The net: the break-even substitution rate</h3><p class="method">Both sides are put on the same base, one dollar of Amazon online sales. <b>Own emissions</b> are what Amazon&rsquo;s buildings and deliveries emit per $1,000 of its sales. <b>Avoided emissions</b> are what the displaced store energy and car trips would have emitted per $1,000 of in-store spending removed. The <b>substitution rate</b> is the number of cents of in-store spending removed per online dollar (the rest of the online dollar is new consumption). The break-even rate is own emissions divided by avoided emissions. Amazon&rsquo;s share of e-commerce cancels out, so no share is applied.</p>');
